# The system keeps up with the live conversation

## Story

As someone helping on a live sales call,
I want the system to follow the conversation as it happens and hand over each thing that's said one piece at a time,
so that no question is ever missed and the same line is never seen twice.

## What this is

Picture a real call in progress — people talking back and forth. This piece is
the system's ears. It keeps up with the live conversation and, whenever a
complete spoken turn has landed, makes that turn available to the rest of the
system, just once. It also notes, as best it can, who was speaking — the
prospect, our own rep, or unknown when it genuinely can't tell — and roughly
when in the call it happened.

## What "done" looks like

- Each complete spoken turn is handed over exactly once, in the order it was
  said. The same line never comes through twice.
- Only complete, settled turns count. A fragment that's still being spoken is
  never passed along as if it were finished.
- Asking "is there anything new?" is always instant. When nothing new has been
  said yet, the honest answer is simply "nothing right now" — it must never
  make the rest of the system stop and wait for it.
- If the microphone or the connection to the listening service hiccups or
  drops, the system quietly keeps trying to recover on its own. Through the
  whole outage it just keeps answering "nothing right now." It never crashes
  and never pushes the problem back onto whoever asked.
- For rehearsal and testing without any live audio or network, there's a
  practice mode that replays a fixed little script: first the prospect asking
  whether we're SOC 2 certified, then a follow-up asking whether that's Type I
  or Type II, then some weekend small talk, then a pricing question — in that
  order, each one a moment later in the call than the last. Every line comes
  through once; after the script runs out, it's "nothing right now" from then
  on.
- Every time it's asked for the next turn, it leaves one short, quiet note in
  the running log about what came through.

It should never crash, never make the caller wait, never repeat a line, and
never pass along a half-finished fragment.
